{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>And Laban came into Jacob's tent, and into Leah's tent, and into the tent of the two maid-servants.</b> For there was one tent for both of them, or [he came] into the tent of each of them and afterward returned a second time to Leah's tent, and the witness is 'and he went out from Leah's tent.' Also plausible is that Jacob's tent was between Leah's tent and Rachel's tent. But what seems closest to me is that Scripture took a short path and delayed mentioning Rachel's tent. Thus he came into Jacob's tent, then into Leah's tent, then into Rachel's tent, then into the tent of the two maid-servants. The reason [Scripture mentions only these three] is that he searched in Jacob's tent and in Leah's tent and in the tent of the two maid-servants and found nothing, for there was nothing there; and at the time he went out from Leah's tent and came into Rachel's tent, Rachel had already taken the teraphim and put them in the camel's saddle."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {0 "Resolved אח\"כ and שהי' as standard medieval abbreviations for אחר כך ('afterward') and שהיה ('that was')."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:46:39.987944592Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}